{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791624250,"nonce":"FutAYcnqVJBofV9tBNf/0cuQQPg=","title":"Keep transform and opacity animations smooth while the main thread is busy","message":"Asked 2026-10-10 whether animation can run off the main thread, as ashui's AnimationScheduler.run does (a sys.thread stepping springs every 1/120 s under a mutex; the main thread still renders and only reads the values).\n\nThat design does not carry over. blinc_ts motion is sampled by time: each frame computes value(now) natively (native/src/motion), so a busy main thread drops frames but never falls behind, and nothing needs stepping between frames. worker_threads cannot reach the layout tree, whose native objects belong to the main isolate. A Rust thread in the addon could tick, but apply_paint and the TypeGPU encoding both run on the main thread, so it would add locking without smoother frames.\n\nWhat would help is what browsers do for compositor-only animations: a native thread that re-presents the last encoded frame with only transform and opacity changed, while JavaScript is blocked. That needs the frame retained as layers on the GPU with per-layer transform and opacity uniforms, a presenter that can run without the main thread (xgpu surface access off-main), and a hand-off of in-flight transform/opacity runs from the engine to that thread. Worth doing once the wiki shows jank under load; measure first with the window motion test (tests/native-motion-window.mjs) under a blocked main thread.","files":null}]}